1
00:00:03,271 --> 00:00:09,875
On 5 June 1989, a man in a white shirt
walked into a wide avenue in Beijing,

2
00:00:10,266 --> 00:00:11,979
and stopped a column of tanks.

3
00:00:13,891 --> 00:00:17,459
Nobody has ever proved who he
was, or what happened to him.

4
00:00:19,151 --> 00:00:22,070
This is the story of the spring
that led to that moment,

5
00:00:22,461 --> 00:00:24,859
and of what the world
could see, and could not.

6
00:00:29,631 --> 00:00:31,044
It began with a funeral.

7
00:00:31,455 --> 00:00:34,693
Hu Yaobang, a Communist
Party leader seen as a reformer,

8
00:00:35,084 --> 00:00:37,440
died on 15 April 1989.

9
00:00:39,092 --> 00:00:42,118
Students gathered in Tiananmen
Square to mourn him.

10
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:49,020
This is where, forty years ago, Mao
Zedong stepped into the place
previously reserved for emperors,

11
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:53,620
handing down their edicts, and
proclaimed the triumph of his
communist revolution.

12
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:57,620
Tiananmen Square is the heart of
China.

13
00:00:59,251 --> 00:01:00,487
The mourning was allowed.

14
00:01:00,919 --> 00:01:02,979
The demands that followed were not.

15
00:01:04,691 --> 00:01:08,773
Their grief soon turned into a protest
against corruption and rising prices,

16
00:01:09,044 --> 00:01:10,739
and a call for political reform.

17
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:14,660
But then their lecturer shot them a
zinger,

18
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:20,760
reminding them of the charge that high
government and party officials
enriched their own families at the
expense of the people.

19
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:25,060
If you want to solve corruption, he
said, begin with your own sons.

20
00:01:26,831 --> 00:01:29,629
On 26 April, the party newspaper,

21
00:01:29,799 --> 00:01:33,139
People's Daily, published an
editorial about the protest.

22
00:01:35,011 --> 00:01:37,067
The editorial said the protest was,

23
00:01:37,198 --> 00:01:40,578
in its words, a planned
conspiracy and a disturbance.

24
00:01:42,551 --> 00:01:45,112
Many students took that
as an insult to their grief,

25
00:01:45,383 --> 00:01:46,578
and the crowds got bigger.

26
00:01:48,472 --> 00:01:51,218
The leadership was
divided on how to respond.

27
00:01:52,951 --> 00:01:56,251
Zhao Ziyang, the general
secretary, wanted talks.

28
00:01:56,522 --> 00:01:58,599
Hardliners wanted the protest ended.

29
00:02:00,552 --> 00:02:03,438
In May, some students
began a hunger strike.

30
00:02:05,231 --> 00:02:08,118
The Soviet leader, Mikhail Gorbachev,

31
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:12,399
was about to arrive for the first Soviet
and Chinese summit in thirty years.

32
00:02:14,371 --> 00:02:16,571
Foreign reporters had
come to cover the summit.

33
00:02:16,882 --> 00:02:18,439
They stayed to cover the Square.

34
00:02:20,291 --> 00:02:24,092
The crowds grew beyond students,
to workers, teachers and families.

35
00:02:24,503 --> 00:02:26,739
Some reports put them at
more than a million people.

36
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:32,380
They defied a government order to be
out of the square by the time
Gorbachev arrived,

37
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:34,780
and no one seemed afraid.

38
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:37,280
Student: Why should I be afraid?

39
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:40,880
I'm just a common student.

40
00:02:42,651 --> 00:02:46,203
On 19 May, the party
leader, Zhao Ziyang,

41
00:02:46,223 --> 00:02:48,259
went to the Square to
see the hunger strikers.

42
00:02:53,071 --> 00:02:56,657
On 20 May, the government
declared martial law in Beijing.

43
00:02:58,793 --> 00:03:01,179
Troops began to move
toward the centre of the city.

44
00:03:02,713 --> 00:03:04,097
The protest did not end.

45
00:03:05,791 --> 00:03:09,191
In the last days of May, students
raised a statue in the Square.

46
00:03:09,823 --> 00:03:11,839
They called it the
Goddess of Democracy.

47
00:03:13,911 --> 00:03:16,992
It stood in front of the portrait
of Mao, facing the gate.

48
00:03:17,504 --> 00:03:19,159
The government called it an insult.

49
00:03:20,971 --> 00:03:24,679
On the night of 3 June, the army
moved on the centre of the city.

50
00:03:26,271 --> 00:03:28,097
In the early hours of 4 June,

51
00:03:28,117 --> 00:03:31,779
soldiers entered the Square area
with tanks and armoured vehicles.

52
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:39,200
Tiananmen Square of course is firmly
in the hands of the military,

53
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:46,700
and what once was this country's
symbol of power is now its symbol of
tragedy and shame.

54
00:03:48,612 --> 00:03:51,318
Soldiers fired on people in the
streets around the Square.

55
00:03:53,253 --> 00:03:55,957
No one has ever established
how many people died.

56
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:05,220
We don't know to this moment how many
people were killed and wounded in the
army's attack on the people of
Beijing,

57
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:07,920
the number is certainly in the
hundreds.

58
00:04:09,771 --> 00:04:12,501
A State Department situation
report listed estimates

59
00:04:12,521 --> 00:04:14,718
from five hundred to
twenty six hundred.

60
00:04:16,611 --> 00:04:17,735
The Chinese government said

61
00:04:17,755 --> 00:04:19,891
that more than two
hundred people were killed,

62
00:04:20,604 --> 00:04:22,078
thirty six of them students.

63
00:04:24,131 --> 00:04:27,852
A United States State Department
summary at the time gave a range

64
00:04:28,263 --> 00:04:30,479
of one hundred eighty to five hundred.

65
00:04:32,352 --> 00:04:34,469
Other estimates run
into the thousands.

66
00:04:34,941 --> 00:04:36,938
None of these figures
has been confirmed.

67
00:04:38,851 --> 00:04:42,372
Foreign broadcasters had lost their
satellite links under martial law.

68
00:04:42,964 --> 00:04:44,839
Reporters phoned in what they could.

69
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:51,240
Forest, night has fallen here. It's
awfully quiet at the moment.

70
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:56,240
In the afternoon we were able to hear
some firing out in the west.

71
00:04:56,940 --> 00:04:58,540
The source of it is very uncertain.

72
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:02,440
We cannot say for sure whether it was
between rival army units.

73
00:05:07,071 --> 00:05:11,052
On 5 June, a column of tanks rolled
east along Chang'an Avenue,

74
00:05:11,424 --> 00:05:13,239
the wide road beside the Square.

75
00:05:15,191 --> 00:05:18,531
A man in a white shirt and
dark trousers, carrying bags,

76
00:05:18,822 --> 00:05:20,898
stepped into the road
in front of the first tank.

77
00:05:22,973 --> 00:05:24,117
The tanks stopped.

78
00:05:25,791 --> 00:05:28,429
The Associated Press
counted eighteen tanks,

79
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:31,599
and said the standoff held them
up for about three minutes.

80
00:05:33,411 --> 00:05:35,730
When the lead tank tried
to steer around him,

81
00:05:36,022 --> 00:05:37,719
he moved into its path again.

82
00:05:39,371 --> 00:05:42,078
Then he climbed onto the
tank and spoke to the crew.

83
00:05:43,871 --> 00:05:45,969
How long it lasted
depends on the account,

84
00:05:46,079 --> 00:05:48,538
from about three
minutes to six or seven.

85
00:05:50,411 --> 00:05:52,930
Then people came out of the
crowd and led him away.

86
00:05:53,382 --> 00:05:55,319
Witnesses disagree
about who they were.

87
00:05:56,971 --> 00:05:59,040
The scene was
photographed and filmed

88
00:05:59,060 --> 00:06:01,159
from hotel balconies and rooftops.

89
00:06:02,952 --> 00:06:05,257
Television networks
carried it around the world.

90
00:06:07,191 --> 00:06:09,368
That Monday, President George H.

91
00:06:09,579 --> 00:06:09,679
W.

92
00:06:09,699 --> 00:06:12,499
Bush suspended American
arms sales to China.

93
00:06:14,351 --> 00:06:17,238
Bush said that the image
would be with us a long time.

94
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:23,960
All of us have seen the bravery and
the determination of the students and
the workers,

95
00:06:24,860 --> 00:06:28,060
and all I can say to him, wherever he
might be,

96
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:29,560
or to the people around the world,

97
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:33,960
is we are and we must stand with him.

98
00:06:38,611 --> 00:06:39,764
About two weeks later,

99
00:06:39,854 --> 00:06:43,686
the Sunday Express in London
named him as Wang Weilin,

100
00:06:43,706 --> 00:06:44,939
a nineteen year old student.

101
00:06:46,672 --> 00:06:48,578
That name has never been confirmed.

102
00:06:50,272 --> 00:06:52,558
Inside China, the image is censored.

103
00:06:54,251 --> 00:06:56,968
Party documents, reported
by a Hong Kong rights group,

104
00:06:57,399 --> 00:07:00,858
said officials could not find him
among the dead or the prisoners.

105
00:07:02,911 --> 00:07:06,518
In 1990, Jiang Zemin,
then the party leader,

106
00:07:06,538 --> 00:07:09,233
was asked on American
television what became of him.

107
00:07:09,825 --> 00:07:11,979
He said he thought the
man was never killed.

108
00:07:13,831 --> 00:07:16,471
Ten years later he said the
man was never arrested,

109
00:07:16,542 --> 00:07:18,279
and that he did not
know where he was.

110
00:07:20,051 --> 00:07:21,907
Other accounts say he was executed.

111
00:07:22,278 --> 00:07:23,672
Others say he is alive.

112
00:07:24,224 --> 00:07:25,679
No account has been proved.

113
00:07:27,571 --> 00:07:28,924
On 21 June,

114
00:07:29,075 --> 00:07:30,779
a State Department
summary reported

115
00:07:30,799 --> 00:07:32,875
more than fifteen
hundred people arrested,

116
00:07:33,146 --> 00:07:34,499
among them student leaders.

117
00:07:36,331 --> 00:07:39,790
Washington suspended military
sales and high level contacts,

118
00:07:40,362 --> 00:07:42,579
and Congress pressed
for wider sanctions.

119
00:07:44,711 --> 00:07:46,557
The United States has since called

120
00:07:46,577 --> 00:07:49,175
on the Chinese government
to account for those killed,

121
00:07:49,286 --> 00:07:50,499
detained or missing.

122
00:07:52,232 --> 00:07:55,599
A man stood in front of the tanks, and
then he vanished from the record.

123
00:07:57,252 --> 00:07:59,377
Who was he, and what
happened to him?
